{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-168862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2}) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-5{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+10\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-5{x}^{2}\\cdot 2y+10\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4y^3-10x^2y+20y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2)=-10x{y}^{2}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,-10x{y}^{2}-4x\\, & \\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168863" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168863" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10xy^2-4x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum .\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/d19c97886c1449b414939269d9f9bf5d7ca8b418268ed5ef9b5940acaf29dbf7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168864" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168864" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-10x{y}^{2}-4x & \\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-10x{y}^{2}-4x=0 \\\\ -4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2x(5{y}^{2}+2)=0 \\\\ -2y(2{y}^{2}+5{x}^{2}-10)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5{y}^{2}+2\\ne 0,$ from the first equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y(2{y}^{2}+5{x}^{2}-10) & =0 \\\\ -2y(2{y}^{2}+5(0)^{2}-10) & =0 \\\\ -4y({y}^{2}-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168865" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-10x{y}^{2}-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y.\n$$\n\nComputing the second partial derivatives, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x{y}^{2}-4x)=-10{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x{y}^{2}-4x)=-20xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y)=-12{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10y^2-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12y^2-10x^2+20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168866" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-20xy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10y^2-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-12y^2-10x^2+20$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-20xy$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nEvaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10{y}^{2}-4 & -20xy \\\\ -20xy & -12{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=[\\begin{aligned}-54 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=(-54)\\cdot (-40)-0\\cdot 0=2\\,160.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=(-4)\\cdot 20-0\\cdot 0=-80.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2160"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168867" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-80"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4y^3-10x^2y+20y$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $[$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10xy^2-4x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y]$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/assets/23b3e3c6e8eeecb32b38e868114bd2967882c5542edcfbee7f31f6e4a26d22da.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Given a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local maximum of the function?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        A local maximum is the point \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        A                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe critical points of $f$ are$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\pm $ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{5}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)$ and $($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n, 0$).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10y^2-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-12y^2-10x^2+20$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-20xy$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nEvaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2160$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-80$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nClassify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ are local maximum since $\\,H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n* If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ \n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n* $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ are local maximum of $f$ since $H(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=2\\,160>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})=-54<0.$\n\n* $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-80<0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2ba5614901a54651b50590c585ba1d18ef45d0e5a35f770658efcc859a5617c"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math ">"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428b8f2761d6170ae511437a6df19c909871"} {"question" "q-168868" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071276/state.json" "file_sha256" "648e8e2710c1b5b1ef937815eaf4428